<p>Coe (center with hat) posing with Robert and Minnie Lange and mine workers c. 1906 Coe had other business interests in the area. Henry Waldo Coe was the owner and operator of the Mount St. Helen's Consolidated Mining Company. Copper, gold and silver plus an assortment of non-metal minerals were discovered in commercial quantities on east side of the Spirit Lake, in the shadow of Mt. St. Helens. Mining peaked in the area between 1902 through 1908.His involvement in the Washington mining industry is highlighted in the Mt. St. Helens Visitor Center operation by the US Park Service.</p>

<p>Coe served in the Oregon state senate in 1894 and later as Oregon's US Senator. Dr. Coe served as president of the City and County Medical Association and was a professor of medicine at Willamette University in Salem, OR. He was one of the party leaders of the national Progressive Party (i.e. "Bull Moose Party") in the early 1900s. He served a president of Oregon's Roosevelt League. He and his wife would be invited on numerous occassions to lunch at the White House, as guests of both President Theodore Roosevelt and Howard Taft.</p>

